     9	 * not prove its own claims about call resolution or method classification (passes 34, 35). Six
    10	 * patterns, one class: the guarantee was distributed across senders, so proving it meant proving a
    11	 * property of every sender, and every proof was weaker than it read.
    12	 *
    13	 * A boundary does not have that problem. If exactly one function may reach the network, then "is the
    14	 * payload checked" stops being a question about six call sites and becomes a question about one.
    15	 *
    16	 * WHAT IT CHECKS, and why here rather than earlier. It reads the request as Telegram will read it —
    17	 * the query string and the serialised body, the exact bytes about to go on the wire. Every earlier
    18	 * placement checked a representation that something later could still change: the caller's text, then
    19	 * the formatter's output, then the object handed to fetch. This is the last one.
    20	 *
    21	 * WHAT PASS 36 FOUND, because moving a boundary is not the same as closing a class:
    22	 *
    23	 *   1. The door checked ONLY a non-empty string body. Telegram accepts parameters in the URL query,
    24	 *      as form encoding, and as multipart — so a reader-visible method sent any of those other ways
    25	 *      reached the network unchecked. The door was one encoding wide.
    26	 *   2. Method and host recognition were case-SENSITIVE while Telegram's dispatch is not. `sendmessage`
    27	 *      is dispatched by Telegram and missed the field map, which returns silently on an unknown key.
    28	 *   3. Moving the boundary DELETED the closed-world method check the previous per-sender lint
    29	 *      performed. A newly-used reader-visible method passed through the approved door, received no
    30	 *      decision, and left while the lint stayed clean — a guard that nothing guards, which is the
    31	 *      exact defect this file's own header claimed to have eliminated.
    32	 *
    33	 * All three share one root: the door treated what it could not check as fine. The rule below is the
    34	 * opposite, and it is the only rule here that matters — AN UNCHECKABLE REQUEST IS A CLOSED DOOR.
    35	 *
    36	 * WHAT THIS DOOR DOES NOT COVER, decided rather than missed (review pass 38 finding 4). A non-Bot URL
    37	 * that REDIRECTS into a Bot API method is classified once, on the initial URL, and `fetch` follows the
    38	 * redirect without a second decision. Manual redirect handling would close it and would also break the
    39	 * file-download callers that legitimately redirect.
    40	 *
    41	 * It is left open because it is outside this guard's purpose: the guarantee is that THIS AGENT does not
    42	 * send a message a reader receives as nothing. A redirect crossing into the Bot API requires an actor
    43	 * who already controls a response this agent fetches — at which point the invisible-payload guard is not
    44	 * the control that matters. Stated so the next reader can disagree with the judgment rather than
