   356	  }
   357	
   358	  if (method !== null) {
   359	    // CLOSED WORLD. `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible` returns silently for a method it has no field for,
   360	    // which is right for a method KNOWN to carry no reader-visible field and wrong for one nobody has
     1	/**
     2	 * invisible-payload.ts — the single definition of "this message has nothing a human can see".
     3	 *
     4	 * Extracted 2026-08-09 because review pass 9 found the regression test had its OWN copy of the
     5	 * predicate: deleting the guard from the route would have left the test green. A test that cannot
     6	 * fail when the thing it guards is removed is the *alive-but-inert* shape in test form — it reports
     7	 * identically whether or not the protection exists. One definition, imported by both.
     8	 *
     9	 * The rule itself is Unicode's, not a hand-written list. An earlier version enumerated five code
    10	 * points and pass 8 immediately produced five more it missed (U+200E, U+2061, U+FE0F, U+00AD,
    11	 * U+180E), so "invisible payloads are refused" was true of the incident and false as a claim.
    12	 * `Default_Ignorable_Code_Point` is the standard's own category for characters that render as
    13	 * nothing; `Cf` is format controls.
    14	 *
    15	 * Earned from a live incident: a peer agent's relay accepted a send whose entire body was one
    16	 * ZERO-WIDTH SPACE, failed with a 500 carrying an EMPTY error body, burned nine retries across
    17	 * 4h17m, and emitted a user-facing "I had a reply for you but couldn't deliver it" notice. There
    18	 * was no reply.
    19	 *
    20	 * **What this does NOT decide:** whether a visible message is *worth* sending. It answers exactly
    21	 * one question — is there anything here a reader could see — and a message consisting of a single
    22	 * full stop passes, correctly.
    23	 */
    24	
    25	/**
    26	 * CONTENT is defined POSITIVELY: a letter, a number, punctuation, or a symbol.
    27	 *
    28	 * ── Why positive, after a round-6 convergence finding proved the subtractive version wrong ──────
    29	 * The original predicate SUBTRACTED a list of invisible classes (whitespace + Default_Ignorable + Cf)
    30	 * and treated everything remaining as visible. That is an OPEN WORLD, and an open world cannot be
    31	 * complete: an external reviewer named the gap and execution confirmed it — a payload consisting only
    32	 * of a C0 control (U+0001, U+0007, U+001B), an unassigned code point (U+0378), a private-use code
    33	 * point (U+E000), a noncharacter (U+FFFE), a lone combining mark (U+0301), or a lone surrogate
    34	 * (U+D800) ALL PASSED as "visible" and would have been delivered. Every one renders as nothing or as
    35	 * tofu — the exact harm of the incident this guard exists for, on a wider input surface.
    36	 *
    37	 * Subtracting the invisible is the same mistake as enumerating the senders: you can only ever remove
    38	 * the shapes you thought of. Naming what COUNTS closes the world — anything not named is not content,
    39	 * including whatever Unicode adds next.
    40	 *
    41	 * `L` letters, `N` numbers, `P` punctuation (so a lone full stop is a legitimate message, as it always
    42	 * was), `S` symbols (so emoji count).
    43	 *
    44	 * **ALL marks (`M`) are content, and the road here is worth recording because I was wrong twice.**
    45	 * The first version excluded every mark. Review pass 30 proved that over-refuses real text and I admitted
    46	 * `Mc`/`Me`, justifying the split by ADVANCE WIDTH — spacing and enclosing marks occupy width, nonspacing
    47	 * marks do not.
    48	 *
    49	 * **That justification was false, and review pass 31 refuted it by measurement.** I re-measured on this
    50	 * host rather than concede on assertion: at 40pt, `Mn` U+20D0 COMBINING LEFT HARPOON ABOVE advances
    51	 * **18.400** and `Mn` U+0301 COMBINING ACUTE advances **15.078**. Nonspacing marks are NOT zero-advance,
    52	 * so General Category was never an advance-width predicate — it was a plausible story fitted to two
    53	 * examples. (I do not reproduce the reviewer's `Me` = 0.000 figure; mine measures 42.695. That
    54	 * discrepancy is beside the point — the half that refutes MY claim reproduces.)
    55	 *
    56	 * So the split is gone rather than re-justified. Unicode classes `M` as GRAPHIC; a mark renders, on its
    57	 * base or on a dotted-circle placeholder. Admitting all of `M` is also the SAFE direction: this guard
    58	 * exists to stop invisible sends, and every over-refusal it makes destroys a real message.
    59	 *
    60	 * `L` letters, `N` numbers, `P` punctuation (so a lone full stop is a legitimate message, as it always
    61	 * was), `S` symbols (so emoji count), `M` marks. Deliberately NOT content: separators (`Z`) and every
    62	 * control/format/unassigned/private-use/surrogate category (`C`).
