docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:15:3. **DESIGN — Native `fetch` URL and Request inputs bypass the string-only classifier at runtime.** `telegramFetch` narrows its argument to `string` at [telegram-egress.ts:197](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:197), but native `fetch` accepts strings, `URL` objects, and `Request` objects. For either object form, `.trim()` throws and is converted to `null` at [telegram-egress.ts:74](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:74), after which the object is forwarded unchanged at line 245. A `Request`’s body is also absent from `init.body`, so no parameter collection occurs. The TypeScript signature prevents ordinary typed calls, but the emitted JavaScript boundary performs no runtime refusal; an invisible Bot API request represented by either native input shape leaves unchecked.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:17:4. **DESIGN — Redirects can cross into the Bot API after the only classification decision.** The initial URL is classified once at [telegram-egress.ts:198](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:198); `method === null` is explicitly permitted, and `fetch` is invoked with its normal redirect behavior at [telegram-egress.ts:245](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:245). Failure path: a non-Bot initial URL returning a body-preserving redirect to a reader-visible Bot API method crosses the network with no second classification or payload check. Nothing constrains `telegramFetch` to file-download URLs or uses manual redirect handling.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:21:6. **PRECISION — The spec and funnel comments still describe the deleted per-sender architecture as live.** The “read before citing” summary still calls the guard interim and presence-only at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:50](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:50). Its lint section claims the current boundary lint reads the method map and maintains a sender ratchet at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:141](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:141), and the decision table repeats that deleted population model at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:308](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:308). The actual lint only locates direct Bot-host `fetch` calls at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:122](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:122); it reads neither runtime classification table. The Lifeline likewise says the lint enforces passage through its private `apiCall` at [TelegramLifeline.ts:2907](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2907), which it does not. Failure path: a maintainer trusts the claimed private-funnel or method-table enforcement, changes that layer, and receives a clean lint because the lint proves only passage through `telegramFetch`.
