docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass39-verdict.md:87:1. **Still open, but incompletely described.** The malformed-tag over-refusal and encoded-invisible/emphasis under-refusals remain at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:312-337`. The explicitly allowed valid-pure-markup case at `:363-379` is a third known under-refusal: valid markup with no text node renders as nothing and is deliberately sent.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass39-verdict.md:91:3. **Still open as described.** Runtime property escapes remain at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:64` and `:77`; the vendored table remains assigned to CMT-1261 at `:83-89`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:21:   The raw-HTML send path intentionally retries malformed HTML as plain text, explicitly noting that the tags then become visible: `src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:1400-1412`. But `readerVisibleText()` strips every tag-shaped substring before Telegram validates it: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:279-283`. Therefore, for an HTML input containing no text-node content but containing tag syntax that Telegram would reject, the new check throws before the first request. The plain-text recovery path never runs, even though that path would have shown the tag text to the reader.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:23:   This is new blocking behaviour introduced by the repair. It conflicts with the stated guard policy that an over-refusal destroys a message: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:56-58`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:27:   The HTML branch removes tags but does not decode character references: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:281-283`. In HTML passthrough mode, the formatter deliberately returns the caller’s HTML unchanged: `src/messaging/TelegramMarkdownFormatter.ts:532-541`. Consequently, a numeric character reference resolving only to a default-ignorable character is counted from its source punctuation and digits, although Telegram renders the decoded invisible character.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:29:   The Markdown branch recognizes only inline links: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:284-286`. Yet legacy-passthrough preserves the caller’s Markdown representation byte-for-byte: `src/messaging/TelegramMarkdownFormatter.ts:497-506`, `src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:5862-5875`. Formatting delimiters outside the link pattern can therefore supply the category-positive source characters while Telegram consumes them as markup.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:31:   The reasoning fails at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:260-277`: “reader actually receives” requires Telegram grammar and decoding, whereas the implementation provides two regular-expression projections.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:37:   It never checks `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible`. Removing the post-format call from the Lifeline at `src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2914` would leave the lint satisfied because the pre-format call at `:2908` remains. The post-format behavioural tests exercise an Adapter configured for Markdown, not the Lifeline: `tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:454-500`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:51:   Methods passed through variables, constants, enums, expressions, aliases, or unrecognized wrappers are not classified. At runtime, unrecognized methods return no reader-visible field and therefore receive no content decision: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:125-139`, `:299-305`, `:326-332`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:59:  - `telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts`
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:74:- It follows HTML through the pre-request guard and the existing post-400 plain-text recovery path. That reveals a new over-refusal, rather than another invisible-payload under-refusal.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:137:- “Reader-visible text” is claimed at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:260-277`, but the implementation omits decoding and most Markdown grammar.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass34-verdict.md:144:The repair closes the demonstrated default-Markdown case, but its “what the reader receives” claim is broader than its logic. It introduces a real send-path regression, leaves representation-dependent invisible payloads unmeasured, and is not itself fully covered by the funnel assurance.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass31-verdict.md:26:   Independently, adding a real `this.apiCall("sendPhoto", …)` also exited clean because closed-world discovery recognizes only single-quoted calls ([lint:227](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-send-funnel-guarded.mjs:227), [lint:234](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-send-funnel-guarded.mjs:234)). These are one root finding: source-text grammar is still being presented as evidence of live calls and complete method classification. It directly violates the acceptance criterion that a decoyed guard must fail ([spec:420](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:420)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass31-verdict.md:30:   The repair says `Mc` and `Me` carry advance width while `Mn` has zero advance, then implements that distinction solely through General Category ([predicate:44](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:44), [predicate:51](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:51), [predicate:57](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:57)).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass31-verdict.md:37:   Injecting U+20D0 into the visible controls made the real guard test fail: `assertTelegramPayloadVisible` threw `InvisiblePayloadRefusedError`. Current fixtures pin only one selected `Mn`, U+0301, while admitting the selected `Mc`/`Me` examples ([test:270](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:270), [test:282](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:282)). General Category is not an advance-width predicate; the repair still over-refuses a rendered graphic mark.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass31-verdict.md:137:The central Telegram runtime is also materially improved: tested invisible payloads are suppressed at all three agent funnels, ordinary payloads deliver, and refused operations now emit one record.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass42-verdict.md:23:   Concrete failure path: the first read returns `undefined`, so no reader-visible field is checked. The spread’s second read returns a serialized invisible body, which remains in the outgoing init and reaches `fetch` unchecked.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass42-verdict.md:41:4. **PRECISION — freezing changes `URLSearchParams` and multipart representation.**
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass42-verdict.md:45:   Both wrappers become plain strings. Native fetch normally assigns URL-encoded or boundary-bearing multipart media types; the replacement receives `text/plain` without explicit headers. `FormData` file parts are also dropped. Telegram selects parsing from the request encoding. [Telegram Bot API request documentation](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#making-requests).
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass42-verdict.md:47:   Concrete failure path: an ordinary visible mutable-wrapper request is inspected as fields, then transmitted as plain text Telegram will not parse as that encoding. The “DELIVERS a multipart body” test proves only that its fetch mock was called; it never checks the outgoing body or media type.
