docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass27-verdict.md:57:**4. SEVERITY: major (load-bearing) — PRE-EXISTING. The invisible-payload guard is not at the door its reader-facing account names.**
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass27-verdict.md:93:**Runtime source, not registry machinery.** Twenty-six passes examined `scripts/` and `docs/`. The delta also contains the invisible-payload module and a block in the server routes. I traced the guard's reach by import, asked "what else calls `sendToTopic` with an operator-supplied body?", and then **booted a probe server** rather than reasoning about it. **New probe nobody has tried: stand up the route harness and fire the payload at the SIBLING route.**
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:19:3. **major** — The invisible-payload regression test still does not regression-test the route. Proven by execution: deleting the `if (hasNoVisibleCharacters(text)) { … }` block from `src/server/routes.ts:14925` in the clone leaves `tests/unit/telegram-reply-invisible-payload.test.ts` green (4 passed). Only two files reference the predicate (`grep` over all `.ts/.js/.mjs/.cjs`), and the test never constructs the app. The repo already ships the stronger remedy pass 9 named first — `tests/unit/localhost-link-guard-route.test.ts` is a ~60-line `createRoutes(ctx)` + express harness testing a deterministic guard on this exact route — and it was not copied. The file's own header calls itself a "Regression test for the invisible-payload refusal on POST /telegram/reply/:topicId"; it is not one.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:57:Not declining on either series. Pass 9's single dip did not hold. More decisive than the level: each of the last four passes has found a **new** load-bearing defect introduced by the immediately preceding repair — pass 7 found the `--update-baseline` writer erasing the chain it was protecting; pass 8 found the invisible-payload guard covering one code point instead of the class; pass 9 found the regression test detached from production; pass 10 finds the collision refusal covering one half of the collision space while certifying it covers the whole. The repair rate and the defect-introduction rate inside the repairs are matched.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass10-verdict.md:72:**No.** Seven specific mismatches between the repository's account of itself and its machinery, all verified by execution: (i) `STANDARDS-REGISTRY.md:682` and `lint-enforcement-fingerprint.mjs:150-152` say the printed count and the verified set are now the same by construction — attack B prints 89/6/82; (ii) the same passage says both guards refuse on their own evidence — for half the collision space only the sibling refuses; (iii) the deferral guard says prose and comments do not resolve a referent — a `.ts` comment after a colon resolves a new marker; (iv) `tests/unit/telegram-reply-invisible-payload.test.ts` calls itself a route regression test — deleting the route's guard leaves it green; (v) `GAP-no-moment-declared.outsidePopulation` says 82 and 86 for the same quantity in adjacent fields; (vi) `STANDARDS-REGISTRY.md:170` says 93% and 92% for the same measurement in one paragraph; (vii) the repository's own required coverage gate rejects the submitted state. The registry is markedly more candid than at pass 1 — the falsification notes, the residual sizing, the borrowed-coverage concession are all real and unusual — but candour about past over-claims is not the same as the current account being true, and the current account is not.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:4:Even excluding the acknowledged CMT-1260 under-refusals, the boundary can forward unchecked Telegram requests, the lint does not prove its “single door” claim, and the retained rendering projection still over-refuses valid fallback content. Several source, test, and specification claims also remain false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:11:1. **DESIGN — Alternative Telegram parameter encodings bypass the guard.** The door checks only non-empty string bodies, while every other `BodyInit` and every query-string request falls through directly to `fetch`: [telegram-egress.ts:61](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:61), [telegram-egress.ts:63](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:63), [telegram-egress.ts:78](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:78). Telegram supports URL-query, form-encoded, JSON, and multipart parameters, so the comment that body-carrying methods necessarily use JSON is false: [telegram-egress.ts:54](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:54), [Telegram Bot API request rules](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#making-requests). Failure path: a reader-visible method sent through any supported non-string encoding reaches the network without `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible`.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:13:2. **DESIGN — URL and method recognition are textual, not equivalent to Telegram dispatch.** `BOT_API_URL` is case-sensitive and returns the method spelling unchanged: [telegram-egress.ts:27](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:27), [telegram-egress.ts:38](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:38). The field map is then indexed case-sensitively and an absent entry silently returns: [invisible-payload.ts:125](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:125), [invisible-payload.ts:311](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:311). Telegram method names are case-insensitive, and URL hostnames are case-insensitive. Failure path: an equivalent host or method spelling is dispatched by Telegram while the door derives `null` or an unmapped method and forwards it unchecked.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:15:3. **DESIGN — Moving the boundary deleted closed-world method enforcement.** The source still declares guarded and bodyless sets, but `telegramFetch` never consults the bodyless set or rejects an unknown method; `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible` simply returns when the map has no field: [invisible-payload.ts:142](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:142), [invisible-payload.ts:168](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:168), [invisible-payload.ts:312](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:312). The replacement lint checks only network confinement: [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:92](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:92). Failure path: a newly used reader-visible Bot API method passes through the approved door, receives no content decision, and leaves while lint remains clean. This is the remaining “guard that nothing guards.”
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:19:5. **DESIGN — The retained HTML/Markdown transforms are still parser approximations and still over-refuse.** The HTML regex removes every tag-shaped substring without establishing that Telegram recognizes valid markup: [invisible-payload.ts:274](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:274), [invisible-payload.ts:292](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:292). The “undecidable” escape applies only when extraction is completely empty: [invisible-payload.ts:319](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:319), [invisible-payload.ts:335](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:335). Failure path: malformed or unsupported tag-shaped visible source plus a remaining invisible text node produces a non-empty invisible extraction and terminal refusal at [TelegramAdapter.ts:1435](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:1435), although Telegram’s parse rejection would have activated the plain fallback that displays the source. Thus “true without qualification” and “provably receives nothing” are false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass36-verdict.md:21:6. **PRECISION — The consolidation left the source and specification describing the deleted architecture.** `assertOutgoingPayloadVisible` is still called a deliberately second check beside a pre-format check: [invisible-payload.ts:303](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:303). The same file still says both funnels call the pre-format guard first and that the deleted predecessor lint enforces it: [invisible-payload.ts:156](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:156), [invisible-payload.ts:184](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:184). Adapter and Lifeline comments likewise refer to a nonexistent pre-format call: [TelegramAdapter.ts:5762](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:5762), [TelegramLifeline.ts:2916](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2916). The spec declares CMT-1246 shipped at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:196](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:196), then later describes it as still-open and blocking at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:376](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:376). Failure path: a maintainer trusts nonexistent independent guard and closed-world lint layers while changing the sole remaining enforcement point.
