docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass43-verdict.md:11:The principal new bypass is that Telegram’s `editMessageText` accepts reader-visible `rich_message` content without `text`, while the door checks only `text` and returns silently when it is absent. [Telegram documentation](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#editmessagetext)
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:11:1. **DESIGN — The repeated-parameter repair omitted JSON and multipart.** JSON is collapsed with `JSON.parse` and `Object.assign` at [telegram-egress.ts:142](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:142), retaining the last duplicate property. Multipart likewise overwrites on every part at [telegram-egress.ts:176](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:176). Telegram preserves argument order and its accessor returns the first match ([HttpReader.cpp](https://github.com/tdlib/td/blob/master/tdnet/td/net/HttpReader.cpp), [Query.h](https://github.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/blob/master/telegram-bot-api/Query.h)). The new test at [telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:156](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:156) exercises only query parameters while claiming the general rule. Failure path: in either omitted encoding, an invisible first reader-visible value followed by a visible duplicate makes the door inspect the latter while Telegram sends the former; reversing them causes the symmetric over-refusal.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:19:5. **PRECISION — The governing predicate description still excludes marks that runtime accepts.** Runtime includes `\p{M}` at [invisible-payload.ts:64](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:64). The spec nevertheless defines mechanically-visible as L/N/P/S at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:227](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:227), explicitly says a lone mark does not pass at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:237](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:237), and still bounds cross-engine drift to L/N/P/S at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:320](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:320). This directly contradicts the corrected account at line 306 and the mark fixtures. Failure path: a maintainer derives the wrong accepted population and Unicode-drift surface from the document explicitly designated as the governing definition.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass38-verdict.md:21:6. **PRECISION — The spec and funnel comments still describe the deleted per-sender architecture as live.** The “read before citing” summary still calls the guard interim and presence-only at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:50](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:50). Its lint section claims the current boundary lint reads the method map and maintains a sender ratchet at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:141](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:141), and the decision table repeats that deleted population model at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:308](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:308). The actual lint only locates direct Bot-host `fetch` calls at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:122](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:122); it reads neither runtime classification table. The Lifeline likewise says the lint enforces passage through its private `apiCall` at [TelegramLifeline.ts:2907](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2907), which it does not. Failure path: a maintainer trusts the claimed private-funnel or method-table enforcement, changes that layer, and receives a clean lint because the lint proves only passage through `telegramFetch`.
