   140	
   141	The close-out above was written at hour fifteen, when work was parked on a memory constraint. **That was
   142	wrong, and the operator corrected it:** the constraint was real but not binding. A second machine with
   143	137 GB — eight times the one everything had been running on — was completely idle the entire time. The
   144	deferral was not "we lack capacity"; it was "we never asked whether the capacity could move."
   145	
   146	What that correction bought, all of it after the close-out was written:
   147	
   148	## The convergence stopped being blocked
   149	
   150	It was never blocked by capability. Five real blockers stood in the way, none of them the memory number:
   151	
   152	1. `node` absent from the remote shell's PATH — every credential lookup exited 127, which is the whole
   153	   reason the other agent's lanes looked "unreachable" for hours.
   154	2. The laptop's checkout points at a different remote and cannot authenticate non-interactively, so it
   155	   could not fetch the branch at all. Routed around by shipping the inputs over the existing channel.
   156	3. **The other agent's session API accepted a prompt, recorded it, and handed the underlying tool an
   157	   empty one** — three lanes reported `running`, then `completed` in three minutes having written
   158	   nothing. A lane that reports success while doing nothing is worse than one that fails.
   159	4. The tool is installed as a version-manager shim, invisible to every non-interactive shell.
   160	5. It refuses to run outside a repository without a flag, and waits on stdin that never arrives.
