   172	## What changed since pass 45 — the grammar, built from the type definitions
   173	
   174	The prose page truncated three times; the type definitions did not. What they say:
   175	
   176	- `richTextPlain` holds the literal in `text` (string).
   177	- `richTexts` holds the sequence in `texts` (array of RichText).
   178	- Every wrapper (bold, italic, url, mention, code, ~25 of them) nests a RichText under `text`.
   179	
   180	So `text` is the single carrier at every level and `texts` the only branching point — the recursive walk
   181	IS the grammar rather than an approximation. Block level adds `name` and `expression` as string carriers;
   182	the container adds `html` and `markdown`.
   183	
   184	**Two deliberate judgments to attack:**
   185	1. Destinations are NOT counted as content — `url`, `email_address`, `phone_number`, `anchor_name`,
   186	   `document`. The reader sees the LABEL, which is the nested `text`. Is that right for every wrapper?
   187	2. A structure yielding NO leaves is ALLOWED, on the reasoning that a media-only rich message
   188	   legitimately carries no text. Is there a shape where that reasoning fails?
   189	
   190	**Also still open and NOT claimed:** structured content is inspected only in its JSON-body
   191	representation; the lint's `fetch.call`/`fetch.apply` receiver check; the token-root `method` fallback
   192	characterised more broadly than it is.
   193	
   194	
   195	
   196	## What changed since pass 46
   197	
   198	**RichText is a UNION** — a bare `string`, an `array` of RichText, or one of 22 wrapper interfaces, and
   199	the same union serves input and output (so the input-vs-output question is settled). The **bare-string
   200	arm** is what every previous version lost: in `{"text": ["hello", {...}]}` the literal is an ARRAY
   201	ELEMENT, under no key, and a key-based walk returns early on it. Collected now.
   202	
   203	Also: `summary` on a details block added; the depth bound raised from 16 (which truncated legitimate
   204	nesting) to 200; a block anchor's `name` REMOVED as a carrier, since it is a jump target rather than
   205	rendered text.
   206	
   207	**RECORDED, NOT FIXED — attack this if you think the disposition is wrong.** The key `name` is a
   208	non-rendered jump target on `RichTextAnchor` and a DISPLAYED label on `RichTextReference`. Same key,
   209	opposite answers, so no key rule is right for both. It is excluded — safe for delivery, under-counts a
   210	reference label, so a reference whose label is its only visible content is wrongly refused. Resolving it
   211	needs the union's type discriminator.
   212	
   213	**Also open:** cross-field query/body precedence; leaf/no-leaf for invisible tables versus visible media;
   214	normalized host spellings in the boundary lint; structured content inspected only in its JSON-body
   215	representation.
