   213	## CMT-1246 shipped — what the criteria got right, and the one they could not anticipate
   214	
   215	The shared-client alternative above is no longer held back. It shipped on 2026-08-11 as
   216	`src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts`, and the design this spec's title describes — "a Guard Call Present
   217	in Every Derived Sender File" — is superseded by it. The title is left as written because it records
   218	what was approved; this section records what replaced it and why that was already sanctioned here.
   219	
   220	Against the four acceptance criteria, derived rather than asserted:
   221	
   222	- **(a) exactly one function issues an HTTP request to the Telegram API host** — MET.
   223	  `scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs` proves it by parse and fails if any other file reaches
   224	  the Bot API host. It also canary-tests its own URL recogniser before trusting a verdict, because a
   225	  recogniser that silently stops seeing turns a boundary lint into a permanent green light.
   226	- **(c) the lint's assertion becomes a boundary check** — MET. The predecessor lint is deleted.
   227	- **(b) every per-sender guard call is DELETED** — MET SEVEN OF EIGHT, and the eighth cannot be met.
   228	  The tokenless-standby relay egress hands the message to ANOTHER MACHINE; this process never makes a
   229	  request to the Telegram host, so the door cannot see it. Deleting that guard would open the hole the
   230	  spec's own §1 describes. The criterion was authored before that egress's nature was understood, and
   231	  the honest resolution is to name the exception rather than delete a guard to make a checklist even.
   232	  Of the seven removed, two were the funnel pre-format guards: execution showed every payload they
   233	  caught is still refused by the door and only the message changes, which is exactly the double-cover
   234	  the alternatives list rejects.
   235	- **(d) the vendored invisible-codepoint table** — NOT MET. Untouched; the predicate still uses runtime
   236	  `\p{...}` escapes, so it still depends on the host's Unicode version. Tracked as its own commitment
   237	  rather than folded into a claim of completion.
   238	
   239	## One term, used consistently: MECHANICALLY-VISIBLE (a term LOCAL to this spec)
   240	
   241	Round 3 asked for the distinction; round 5 correctly noted the document still slid between the two terms.
   242	**"Mechanically-visible" is this document's own term, not an industry or Unicode concept** — it names a specific implemented predicate and should not be read as a general standard for visibility. One term now carries the guarantee: **a payload is MECHANICALLY-VISIBLE when its reader-facing field
   243	contains at least one LETTER, NUMBER, PUNCTUATION MARK, SYMBOL, or MARK.**
   244	
   245	**That definition is POSITIVE, and round 6 is why.** It was subtractive — remove whitespace,
   246	`Default_Ignorable` and `Cf`, and treat whatever remained as visible. An external reviewer named that as an
   247	open world, and execution confirmed the hole: a payload of only a C0 control (`U+0001`, `U+0007`, `U+001B`),
   248	an unassigned code point, a private-use code point, a noncharacter, or a lone
   249	surrogate **ALL PASSED as visible** and would have been delivered. Every one renders as nothing or as tofu —
   250	the incident's exact harm on a wider input surface.
   251	>
   252	> A LONE COMBINING MARK was originally in that list and has been removed from it. Review passes 30-31
   253	> measured the exclusion as an over-refusal of real text — every mark is graphic and carries advance
   254	> width — so `\p{M}` was admitted and a lone mark is content under the current predicate, deliberately.
   255	> Leaving it listed as a harm made this passage contradict the predicate three sections below it, which
   256	> four consecutive readings flagged before the sweep finally searched the CLAIM rather than its notation.
   257	
   258	**Subtracting the invisible is the same mistake as enumerating the senders: you can only remove the shapes
   259	you thought of.** Naming what COUNTS closes the world, including against whatever Unicode adds next. A mark
   260	attached to a base letter still passes, because the letter is content. **A LONE MARK ALSO PASSES** —
   261	review passes 30-31 measured the exclusion as an over-refusal of real text and admitted `\p{M}`. This
   262	sentence said the opposite until review pass 38 finding 5; it is the third pass to catch this claim in
   263	a spelling the previous sweep did not search, which is the argument for searching the CLAIM rather than
   264	one of its notations.
   265	
   266	**And the positive definition had its OWN false positives, found at round 10 and confirmed by execution:**
   267	five code points that are letters (`Lo`) or symbols (`So`) by General_Category and render as empty space —
   268	HANGUL FILLER, the two HANGUL CHOSEONG/JUNGSEONG FILLERS, HALFWIDTH HANGUL FILLER, and BRAILLE PATTERN
   269	BLANK. Each passed. A message of one HANGUL FILLER is the original incident wearing a letter's category.
   270	They are now subtracted from inside the positive set and pinned by fixtures. **Honest about what that
   271	is:** a subtraction has a tail, so a future category-positive blank code point would pass until it is
   272	added. That residual is accepted deliberately and named rather than papered over; the structural fix is the
   273	vendored, generated table under **CMT-1261**, which DERIVES blankness instead of listing it. That, and only that, is what
   274	the code asserts and what every acceptance criterion here means. Throughout this spec, **"reader-visible"
   275	names the PRODUCT INTENT** — the thing we are trying to protect. **The MECHANICAL GUARANTEE is narrower and
   276	is the only thing the code actually asserts:** the field contains at least one code point in
   277	`\p{L}\p{N}\p{P}\p{S}\p{M}` (marks included since review passes 30-31). Those are not the same claim — an unsupported glyph or a client-specific font can
   278	make a code point that PASSES this positive check still render as nothing to a particular reader, which is
   279	why the guarantee is mechanical rather than perceptual. Where this document says a payload is refused for having "no visible characters", read
   280	it as shorthand for the mechanical predicate; the guarantee has never been client-rendered visibility and
   281	must not be cited as though it were.
   282	
   283	## Signal vs authority
   284	
   285	**This is a HARD-INVARIANT VALIDATOR, and by the doc's own words that class is "not a decision point in the
   286	sense this principle applies to" — so it is exempt from the Signal-vs-Authority authority pattern rather
   287	than an instance of it.** Round 13 was right that calling it "authority" while claiming the exemption
   288	strained the vocabulary in both directions. It rejects malformed input at the API edge; it evaluates nothing
   289	about what a message means. **Structured refusal records are emitted anyway, for auditability rather than
   290	because the authority pattern compels them** — a validator that refuses silently is still a validator whose
   291	over-blocks nobody can find.
   292	
   293	It does refuse on paths that previously had no check: `editMessageText`, both lifeline sends, and forum-topic names had no guard before.
   294	The justification is not "no new authority"; it is grounded in the exception the doc actually names for this shape. **Round 12 was right that the fit was
   295	asserted rather than reconciled**, so it is reconciled here. The doc lists *"a deterministic policy evaluator
   296	for domains so constrained that all inputs can be enumerated"* — and arbitrary Unicode payloads are NOT
   297	enumerated, so that is the WRONG citation and this spec used it for four rounds. The correct one is
   298	**hard-invariant validation**: *"Typing and structural validators at the boundary of the system are not
   299	decision points in the sense this principle applies to — they don't evaluate messages, they reject malformed
   300	input. These belong at the API edge and are fine as brittle blockers."* That is exactly what this is — a
   301	structural validator sitting at the API edge, rejecting a payload that carries no content, evaluating
   302	nothing about what the message MEANS. A message of a single full stop passes,
   303	correctly. The lint's authority is a closed-world format invariant at a dev-process chokepoint.
   304	
   305	**Structured decision logging — the requirement this section MISSED until round 11.** `signal-vs-authority.md`
   306	states it plainly: *"Authorities must log their decisions in a structured form: which signals they received,
   307	what the conversation context was, which rule they applied, and what the outcome was."* This guard is
   308	blocking authority and logged nothing. The gap was raised at round 4, not acted on, and raised again at
   309	round 11 — seeing a finding and not acting on it is worse than not seeing it, and it is recorded that way.
   310	Every refusal now emits `{guard, outcome, method, field, rule, valueLength, engine, unicode}` through an
   311	injectable sink, the record is written BEFORE the throw so a caller that swallows the error cannot also
   312	swallow the record, a throwing sink can never convert a refusal into a delivery, and the payload itself is
   313	NEVER logged — length only, because an invisible payload is still user content. The decision also rides on
   314	the error, so a catcher can record it too.
   315	
   316	**On the doc's "conversation context" field, which this record OMITS — reconciled rather than quietly
   317	dropped (round 12).** The requirement exists so over-blocks and under-blocks become detectable. For a gate
   318	that weighs signals against a conversation, context is the evidence you need to judge whether it decided
   319	well. **This predicate is context-FREE by construction:** its verdict depends on the payload and nothing
   320	else, so no amount of conversational context could make an all-invisible payload the right thing to send,
   321	and none could explain a wrong decision. Recording a topic id would make the log look more compliant
   322	without making a single over-block or under-block more detectable. What DOES make them detectable is
   323	already there — the exact method, field, rule and payload length. And the sink is **injectable** precisely
   324	so a host that HAS correlation context can add it at the boundary where that context actually exists,
   325	rather than plumbing a topic id through six senders and a pure predicate to satisfy a field.
   326	
   327	## Decision points touched
   328	
   329	| decision point | classification | justification |
   330	|---|---|---|
   331	| **Refuse an outbound Telegram payload whose reader-visible field has no visible characters** | `invariant` | **Restated after round 1, and REPLACED after round 6.** The predicate is NOT "is this visible to a reader" — that depends on Unicode version, grapheme clustering, emoji modifiers and the rendering client, and is not settled. Nor is it the subtractive question it originally asked, which let eight non-printing categories through. What is closed and decidable is the POSITIVE question the code now asks: *does this string contain at least one letter, number, punctuation mark, symbol, or MARK* (`\p{L}\p{N}\p{P}\p{S}\p{M}`, resolved by the host engine's Unicode tables). **`\p{M}` was added at review passes 30-31**: excluding all marks over-refused real text, and the advance-width rationale for splitting Mn from Mc/Me was measured false on the host and withdrawn — a lone combining mark is content. This paragraph said L/N/P/S/M only until review pass 36 finding 7 caught the spec describing a predicate the code had stopped implementing. Deterministic for a given engine, no competing signal, and closed against categories nobody has thought of yet. There is no competing signal, no context that changes the answer, and no open-domain judgment about meaning — a single full stop passes, correctly. This is the **hard-invariant validation at the API edge** case (see *Signal vs authority* below, where round 12 corrected an earlier mis-citation of the enumerable-inputs exception), not a judgment point wearing an `invariant` label. |
   332	| **Which Telegram methods carry a reader-visible field** | `invariant`, as a **VERSIONED POLICY TABLE** | **Reclassified after round 1.** Calling this "a fact about the API" was wrong twice over: the Bot API can add or change methods, and the `answerCallbackQuery` exclusion is a product-behaviour judgment (an empty toast is *useful*), not a mechanical one. It is an invariant at RUNTIME — a closed code-defined map with no per-call judgment — but it is policy that carries a review trigger, not a timeless truth. **Review trigger:** any Bot API version bump, or any new `apiCall` method reaching a reader. The pinning test is what makes a silent drift impossible. |
   333	| **Whether a source file is a Telegram body-sender (the lint's population)** | `invariant` | Derived mechanically — builds the API host string AND calls `fetch` AND references a body-carrying method. No semantic judgment; a shrink-only ratchet makes a silent population loss loud. |
   334	
   335	> **Contested in both directions, per the standard.** None of the three is a competing-signals point dressed
   336	> as an invariant: none weighs evidence, none has an "it depends" case, and none can be right or wrong about
   337	> intent — only about characters, a code-defined map, and a mechanical file predicate. Conversely, none is
   338	> misfiled as a judgment-candidate: adding an arbiter to "does this string contain content" would be an LLM
   339	> call on a settled question, which *Intelligence Infers* would rightly refuse.
   340	
   341	## Multi-machine posture
   342	
   343	| surface | posture | note |
   344	|---|---|---|
   345	| the refusal predicate | `unified`, with a NAMED runtime dependency | **Corrected after round 2 — the first version overclaimed.** It is a pure function of the payload, but the predicate resolves through the host engine's Unicode tables, so "identical verdict on identical bytes" holds only while machines share a Unicode data version. **Restated at round 9, which caught this row still describing the SUPERSEDED subtractive design:** the drift that matters is no longer `Default_Ignorable`/`Cf` membership — it is **General_Category membership for `L`/`N`/`P`/`S`**, since those four categories are now what the code asks about. The residual is BOUNDED to code points whose L/N/P/S/M membership changed between Unicode releases (in practice, newly ASSIGNED code points: an unassigned point is not content on either version until it becomes a letter or symbol). An empty string, whitespace, ordinary text and emoji already assigned are decided identically on every version. **Direction of the residual, stated because it decides whether this is dangerous:** an older table has FEWER assigned letters/symbols, so an older machine REFUSES a payload made only of a newly-assigned character that a newer machine would send. That direction is fail-SAFE — the older machine withholds rather than delivering emptiness — which is why this is a named dependency rather than a blocker. **No runtime pin exists to cite, and I checked rather than assumed:** `engines.node` is `>=20.12.0` — a
   346	FLOOR, not a pin — CI runs Node 20, and this development machine runs Node 24, so a version spread is not
   347	hypothetical, it is the current state. The residual above is therefore live, bounded as described, and
   348	un-mitigated by any existing enforcement. Vendoring a generated table removes the dependency entirely and
   349	is the clean fix; it rides **CMT-1261** (it was CMT-1246's criterion (d), which shipped without it) and is NOT claimed here. |
   350	| the method→field map | `unified` | Compile-time constant shipped with the code. |
   351	| the lint | `unified` | Runs in CI and at commit time against the repository, not against machine state. |
   352	
   353	**No surface here is machine-local**, so no `machine-local-justification` marker is claimed — and the
   354	bidirectional check holds: `unified` is feasible for all three, because none is credential-bound or
   355	hardware-bound. The one adjacent thing that IS machine-local — a bot token and the topic ids it namespaces —
   356	is untouched by this change and is not a surface this spec introduces.
   357	
   358	## Frontloaded Decisions
   359	
   360	Every decision that would otherwise stop the build mid-run, resolved here:
   361	
   362	1. **Where the refusal sits** — refuse on the path-tested agent funnels; require guard-call PRESENCE in every derived direct-sender file until CMT-1248 makes them path-tested too. Not at a named "chokepoint" function. Resolved: two egress
   363	   mechanisms in the adapter (`apiCall` and the standby relay), one in the lifeline, four direct senders.
   364	2. **Whether to keep a second copy in `sendToTopic`** — no. Two copies closing the SAME case mask each
   365	   other's tests. The relay guard is not a second copy; it closes a DIFFERENT case, and each is
   366	   independently sabotage-proven.
   367	3. **Whether `answerCallbackQuery` is in scope** — no, on the over-refusal ground stated above.
   368	4. **Whether forum-topic names are in scope** — yes. Same class, different field, and swept inside this
   369	   change rather than left for later.
   370	5. **Throw vs silent drop** — throw. Both funnels already throw on a non-ok response, so it is the
   371	   established contract; a caller that swallows it drops an invisible message, which is the correct outcome.
   372	6. **Whether to raise the lint's population baseline automatically** — no. The ratchet is shrink-only and a
   373	   legitimate increase must be a deliberate edit in the same commit as the new sender.
   374	
   375	**Cheap-to-change-after tags claimed: none.** This ships live rather than dark, so no reversibility claim
   376	is being made and none needs contesting.
   377	
   378	## Open questions
   379	
   380	*(none)*
   381	
   382	## Known-open, tracked outside this spec
   383	
   384	**What a `CMT-` reference guarantees, since the term is local and a reader cannot verify it from this
   385	document** (round 13): a CMT is a row in the agent's durable commitment registry, created through an
   386	authenticated API that REFUSES creation without a follow-through choice — either enrolment in the recurring
   387	beacon with a real deadline, or an explicit written opt-out reason. Each carries an owner, a deadline, and a
   388	status; the beacon re-surfaces open ones on a cadence, and an overdue one is raised rather than forgotten.
   389	Failure becomes visible as an overdue open commitment in that registry, not as a silent lapse. The three
   390	below were created with 2026-08-17 deadlines and their ids were read back from the registry — an earlier
   391	attempt at the same three was REJECTED for lacking deadlines and would otherwise have been reported as
   392	tracked while existing nowhere.
   393	
   394	
   395	Two findings from convergence are real, are NOT closed by this change, and are registered as durable
   396	commitments rather than left as prose:
   397	
   398	- **CMT-1247 — the relay refusal-status mismatch is a protocol bug this guard only makes rarer.** The far
   399	  route answers **400** for a content refusal; `isRelayRefusal` recognises only **422**. The local guard
   400	  means an invisible payload no longer travels that path, but ANY other relay-side content refusal still
   401	  surfaces to a caller as "router unreachable". Normalizing the status/schema across that boundary is the
   402	  fix and it is out of scope here.
   403	- **CMT-1246 — LANDED 2026-08-11.** The paragraph below is the pre-ship reasoning, kept because it
   404	  states why the interim design was knowingly non-structural. Read it as history: the centralized client
   405	  exists (`src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts`), the derivation-plus-ratchet lint it criticises is deleted,
   406	  and the ship's true state against the four acceptance criteria is recorded in *CMT-1246 shipped* above —
   407	  including the one criterion that cannot be met and the one that was not. Review pass 36 finding 6 caught
   408	  this section still reading as though the work were pending, one screen after another section said it had
   409	  shipped; a spec that contradicts itself lets a maintainer trust whichever half suits them.
   410	- **The pre-ship reasoning: the centralized client is RISK RETIREMENT, not cleanup**, and round 5 was right to insist on
   411	  the distinction. The interim design is *knowingly non-structural*: it depends on source-shape derivation
   412	  plus a ratchet, and a ratchet catches a population SHRINKING, never a refactor semantically routing
   413	  around a guard that is still present in the file. **CMT-1246 is TIME-BOUND: its registered check-in is 2026-08-17, and it is the BLOCKING PREREQUISITE for any claim stronger than the one in the title**, and that is a
   414	  standing condition rather than an intention. **Stated consequence if it slips:** the guarantee in
   415	  this spec's title cannot be strengthened — "by presence at every derived sender" stays until CMT-1246
   416	  lands, and any future claim of path-complete egress coverage is unearned until it does. It carries the
   417	  vendored codepoint table with it, which also retires the Unicode dependency.
   418	
   419	## Maturation plan
   420	
   421	This change does **not** ship dark, and the reasoning matters: a dark invisible-payload guard would leave a
   422	live hole open while the fix sat inert, which is the state the incident already demonstrated is costly.
   423	Round 6 and round 10 each found a payload class that WAS being delivered — those are open holes today, not
   424	hypotheticals. It ships live, with the rollback in the section below reachable by deleting a small number of
   425	call sites and one lint entry.
   426	
   427	- **test-agent-live:** deploy to a throwaway agent via `/test-as-self` and drive an invisible payload
   428	  through the adapter's API funnel, its standby relay, and the lifeline funnel, confirming each refuses with
   429	  zero network calls AND that a structured refusal record is emitted. Then send ordinary text through the
   430	  same three and confirm delivery — a guard that refuses everything is not a guard. Verifies the feature is
   431	  alive before any real operator's traffic touches it.
   432	- **dev-agent-live:** live on this agent (echo) first, which is also the multi-machine case and therefore
   433	  the one that exercises the tokenless-standby relay path rather than only the direct-send shortcut. The
   434	  observable signal over 24h: zero user-visible "reply lost" notices whose body was empty, and any structured
   435	  refusal records that DO appear name a real method and field. **A zero-everywhere reading is not a pass** —
   436	  no refusals recorded is equally consistent with the guard never running, so the acceptance signal is the
   437	  fixtures continuing to red under sabotage, not the absence of production refusals.
   438	- **fleet:** after the dev-agent 24h window passes clean, in the next ordinary release. No per-agent flag:
   439	  the guard IS the change, and holding it back per-agent would mean the fleet keeps delivering invisible
   440	  payloads while the fix exists.
   441	- **graduation criterion:** one clean 24h window on this agent with no invisible-payload delivery and no
   442	  over-refusal of legitimate traffic (no report of a real message being rejected), PLUS the sabotage suite
   443	  still reddening on demand — 121 tests, both funnels and the lifeline path each proven to red only their
   444	  own arms. The over-refusal half is the one to watch, because this change widened what gets refused twice
   445	  (non-printing categories at round 6, blank glyphs at round 10).
   446	- **dark-window:** none for the refusal itself, per the reasoning above. The genuinely new-behaviour piece
   447	  carrying risk is the **widening** — categories that previously shipped and now do not — and its control is
   448	  not a dark window but the pinned fixtures plus the structured refusal record, which makes any over-refusal
   449	  visible and attributable the first time it happens rather than after a user reports a missing message.
   450	
   451	## Rollback
   452	
   453	Remove two guard calls in the adapter (funnel + relay), one in the lifeline, four one-line calls in the
   454	other senders, and one entry from the `lint` chain. No migration, no persisted state, no agent-state
   455	repair. Callers would resume delivering invisible payloads — the pre-change behaviour.
   456	
   457	## Acceptance criteria
   458	
   459	1. A payload that is not MECHANICALLY-VISIBLE is refused at **every path-tested agent funnel (proven by input, zero network calls)** and **a guard call is present in every lint-detected direct sender FILE (presence proven; behavioural refusal NOT proven)**. The word "guarded" is reserved throughout for input-proven paths.
   460	   Those are two different strengths and the criterion states both rather than averaging them into one word.
   461	   **Said without hedging: for the four direct command-file senders, nothing today executes an invisible
   462	   payload through their real send function — that is CMT-1248, and until it lands "presence" means the
   463	   call is in the file, nothing more.**
   464	   **Amended twice. Round 3 exposed a gap; round 4 caught the amendment CONTRADICTING the criterion it
   465	   amended — requiring "every derived egress, proven by input" while admitting most were not. Resolved by
   466	   closing the gap where it could be closed and stating the guarantee exactly where it could not.**
   467	
   468	   **Path-tested by input (3 of 3 funnels that carry agent traffic):** the adapter's API funnel, the
   469	   adapter's tokenless-standby relay, and the lifeline's own private funnel — each proven to refuse with
   470	   ZERO network calls and to deliver ordinary text, each sabotage-proven to red only its own arms.
   471	
   472	   **NOT path-tested (4 direct command-file senders — two setup-wizard greetings, a self-test probe, and a
   473	   demo-harness sender):** covered by the lint (the guard is called in the file) and by the shared guard's
   474	   unit tests, NOT by a test driving each real send function. **So the criterion reads, honestly: refusal
   475	   is PROVEN BY INPUT at every funnel carrying agent traffic, and PROVEN BY PRESENCE at the four direct
   476	   senders.** Tracked to closure as **CMT-1248**, not as a marker.
   477	2. Visible payloads still deliver, with the exact text asserted — discrimination, not shouting.
   478	3. Each egress guard is independently covered: removing one reds only its own arms.
   479	4. The over-refusal boundary holds: an empty `answerCallbackQuery` is not refused.
   480	5. The lint fails on a deleted, commented-out, decoyed, or unimported guard, and on a shrunken population —
   481	   each asserting its **specific failure string**, never a bare exit code.
   482	6. Full `lint` chain green; no pre-existing invisible-payload or window-10 test regresses.
   483	7. **Every refusal emits a structured decision** — method, field, rule, outcome, payload LENGTH (never the
   484	   payload), and the deciding engine + Unicode version, since the predicate is engine-resolved. Proven by
   485	   input, including that a throwing sink still refuses.
   486	8. **Boundary code points are pinned by fixtures that run on whatever engine executes them** (added on a
   487	   round-5 finding), so a Unicode-table divergence between the supported floor and the CI version reds a
   488	   test instead of being assumed away. The fixtures cover eight zero-width/ignorable boundary points, SEVEN
   489	   NON-PRINTING code points that the old subtractive predicate delivered (C0 controls, unassigned,
   490	   private-use, noncharacter, lone surrogate), and TEN positive controls that must still pass — a full
   491	   stop, a digit, an emoji,
   492	   a base letter carrying a combining acute, and a letter beside an ideographic space. "Control" here means
   493	   a TEST control, never a Unicode control character. The run records its engine, so a divergence is
   494	   attributable rather than mysterious.
   495	
   496	## Verification performed
   497	
   498	- 28 tests in `tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts`; **90 green** across it plus every
   499	  pre-existing invisible-payload and window-10 behavioural test.
   500	- Both egress guards sabotage-proven: removing the relay guard reds exactly its 7 arms with the funnel arms
   501	  green; removing the funnel guard reds exactly its 7 with the relay arms green.
   502	- All five reviewer escapes on the lint closed and re-proven by specific failure string.
   503	- Full `lint` chain exit 0; `tsc --noEmit` clean.
   504	
   505	## Known limits, stated rather than claimed as covered
   506	
   507	- **SEMANTIC BYPASS is the named residual risk, not a theoretical one.** The lint proves a guard is CALLED
   508	  in a sender file, **not that it sits on the path the send takes** —
   509	  a future refactor can satisfy the lint while routing around the guarded call. This is a real boundary
   510	  weakness, not a cosmetic one, and it is the reason the shared-client alternative below is the correct end
   511	  state rather than a nicety. Nothing in this spec should be read as claiming the lint derives guarded
   512	  egress PATHS; it derives guarded FILES. The per-path guarantee is carried entirely by tests.
   513	- A sender reaching Telegram by some mechanism other than a direct `fetch` to the API host falls outside
   514	  the derived population. The shrink ratchet makes a disappearance visible but cannot pre-empt a new shape.
   515	- Non-Telegram adapters (Slack, WhatsApp, iMessage) are entirely out of scope and are not claimed.
   190	## The guard, continued
   191	
   192	Passes 39-45 ran on the laptop. Findings closed since the close-out include: a request carrying its METHOD
   193	in a parameter; a DNS-root-dot host spelling; escaped duplicate JSON keys; a test-environment root; a
   194	mutable body reference; a **second read** of the body introduced by the very line meant to fix the first;
   195	and a method carrying reader-visible content in more than one field.
   196	
   197	**The near-miss worth keeping:** I judged Telegram's `rich_message` a fabricated field because it postdates
   198	my knowledge, and was one message from reporting a real bypass as a hallucination. Fetching the live
   199	documentation showed it real. Acting on a fake field and discarding a real one were one verification
   200	apart — and the instinct to dismiss it *felt* like rigor.
   201	
   202	Then pass 44 showed the repair had the right field and the **wrong shape**: `rich_message` is a structured
   203	object, my checker tested for a string and returned early, so the closed-world table named a field nothing
   204	inspected. A field listed in the map READS as handled — an omission announces itself, a wrong-shaped
   205	handler hides.
