   205	  the codebase issues an HTTP request to the Telegram API host, proven by the derived population collapsing
   206	  to one file; (b) every per-sender `assertTelegramPayloadVisible` call is DELETED, and the behavioural
   207	  suite still reds when the single remaining guard is removed; (c) the lint's assertion changes from
   208	  "every sender calls the guard" to "exactly one sender exists", which is a boundary check and is
   209	  path-complete by construction; (d) the vendored invisible-codepoint table lands with it, removing the
   210	  runtime Unicode dependency named in the multi-machine table above. Recorded here because a rejected alternative that
   211	  is actually the better design must not disappear into a commit message.
   212	
   213	## CMT-1246 shipped — what the criteria got right, and the one they could not anticipate
   214	
   215	The shared-client alternative above is no longer held back. It shipped on 2026-08-11 as
   216	`src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts`, and the design this spec's title describes — "a Guard Call Present
   217	in Every Derived Sender File" — is superseded by it. The title is left as written because it records
   218	what was approved; this section records what replaced it and why that was already sanctioned here.
   219	
   220	Against the four acceptance criteria, derived rather than asserted:
   221	
   222	- **(a) exactly one function issues an HTTP request to the Telegram API host** — MET.
   223	  `scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs` proves it by parse and fails if any other file reaches
   224	  the Bot API host. It also canary-tests its own URL recogniser before trusting a verdict, because a
   225	  recogniser that silently stops seeing turns a boundary lint into a permanent green light.
   226	- **(c) the lint's assertion becomes a boundary check** — MET. The predecessor lint is deleted.
   227	- **(b) every per-sender guard call is DELETED** — MET SEVEN OF EIGHT, and the eighth cannot be met.
   228	  The tokenless-standby relay egress hands the message to ANOTHER MACHINE; this process never makes a
   229	  request to the Telegram host, so the door cannot see it. Deleting that guard would open the hole the
   230	  spec's own §1 describes. The criterion was authored before that egress's nature was understood, and
   231	  the honest resolution is to name the exception rather than delete a guard to make a checklist even.
   232	  Of the seven removed, two were the funnel pre-format guards: execution showed every payload they
   233	  caught is still refused by the door and only the message changes, which is exactly the double-cover
   234	  the alternatives list rejects.
   235	- **(d) the vendored invisible-codepoint table** — NOT MET. Untouched; the predicate still uses runtime
   236	  `\p{...}` escapes, so it still depends on the host's Unicode version. Tracked as its own commitment
   237	  rather than folded into a claim of completion.
   238	
   239	## One term, used consistently: MECHANICALLY-VISIBLE (a term LOCAL to this spec)
   240	
   241	Round 3 asked for the distinction; round 5 correctly noted the document still slid between the two terms.
   242	**"Mechanically-visible" is this document's own term, not an industry or Unicode concept** — it names a specific implemented predicate and should not be read as a general standard for visibility. One term now carries the guarantee: **a payload is MECHANICALLY-VISIBLE when its reader-facing field
   243	contains at least one LETTER, NUMBER, PUNCTUATION MARK, SYMBOL, or MARK.**
   244	
   245	**That definition is POSITIVE, and round 6 is why.** It was subtractive — remove whitespace,
   246	`Default_Ignorable` and `Cf`, and treat whatever remained as visible. An external reviewer named that as an
   247	open world, and execution confirmed the hole: a payload of only a C0 control (`U+0001`, `U+0007`, `U+001B`),
   248	an unassigned code point, a private-use code point, a noncharacter, or a lone
   249	surrogate **ALL PASSED as visible** and would have been delivered. Every one renders as nothing or as tofu —
   250	the incident's exact harm on a wider input surface.
   251	>
   252	> A LONE COMBINING MARK was originally in that list and has been removed from it. Review passes 30-31
   253	> measured the exclusion as an over-refusal of real text — every mark is graphic and carries advance
   254	> width — so `\p{M}` was admitted and a lone mark is content under the current predicate, deliberately.
   255	> Leaving it listed as a harm made this passage contradict the predicate three sections below it, which
   256	> four consecutive readings flagged before the sweep finally searched the CLAIM rather than its notation.
   257	
   258	**Subtracting the invisible is the same mistake as enumerating the senders: you can only remove the shapes
   259	you thought of.** Naming what COUNTS closes the world, including against whatever Unicode adds next. A mark
   260	attached to a base letter still passes, because the letter is content. **A LONE MARK ALSO PASSES** —
   261	review passes 30-31 measured the exclusion as an over-refusal of real text and admitted `\p{M}`. This
   262	sentence said the opposite until review pass 38 finding 5; it is the third pass to catch this claim in
   263	a spelling the previous sweep did not search, which is the argument for searching the CLAIM rather than
   264	one of its notations.
   265	
   266	**And the positive definition had its OWN false positives, found at round 10 and confirmed by execution:**
   267	five code points that are letters (`Lo`) or symbols (`So`) by General_Category and render as empty space —
   268	HANGUL FILLER, the two HANGUL CHOSEONG/JUNGSEONG FILLERS, HALFWIDTH HANGUL FILLER, and BRAILLE PATTERN
   269	BLANK. Each passed. A message of one HANGUL FILLER is the original incident wearing a letter's category.
   270	They are now subtracted from inside the positive set and pinned by fixtures. **Honest about what that
   271	is:** a subtraction has a tail, so a future category-positive blank code point would pass until it is
   272	added. That residual is accepted deliberately and named rather than papered over; the structural fix is the
   273	vendored, generated table under **CMT-1261**, which DERIVES blankness instead of listing it. That, and only that, is what
   274	the code asserts and what every acceptance criterion here means. Throughout this spec, **"reader-visible"
   275	names the PRODUCT INTENT** — the thing we are trying to protect. **The MECHANICAL GUARANTEE is narrower and
   276	is the only thing the code actually asserts:** the field contains at least one code point in
   277	`\p{L}\p{N}\p{P}\p{S}\p{M}` (marks included since review passes 30-31). Those are not the same claim — an unsupported glyph or a client-specific font can
   278	make a code point that PASSES this positive check still render as nothing to a particular reader, which is
   279	why the guarantee is mechanical rather than perceptual. Where this document says a payload is refused for having "no visible characters", read
   280	it as shorthand for the mechanical predicate; the guarantee has never been client-rendered visibility and
   281	must not be cited as though it were.
   282	
   283	## Signal vs authority
   284	
   285	**This is a HARD-INVARIANT VALIDATOR, and by the doc's own words that class is "not a decision point in the
   286	sense this principle applies to" — so it is exempt from the Signal-vs-Authority authority pattern rather
   287	than an instance of it.** Round 13 was right that calling it "authority" while claiming the exemption
   288	strained the vocabulary in both directions. It rejects malformed input at the API edge; it evaluates nothing
   289	about what a message means. **Structured refusal records are emitted anyway, for auditability rather than
   290	because the authority pattern compels them** — a validator that refuses silently is still a validator whose
   291	over-blocks nobody can find.
   292	
   293	It does refuse on paths that previously had no check: `editMessageText`, both lifeline sends, and forum-topic names had no guard before.
   294	The justification is not "no new authority"; it is grounded in the exception the doc actually names for this shape. **Round 12 was right that the fit was
   295	asserted rather than reconciled**, so it is reconciled here. The doc lists *"a deterministic policy evaluator
   296	for domains so constrained that all inputs can be enumerated"* — and arbitrary Unicode payloads are NOT
   297	enumerated, so that is the WRONG citation and this spec used it for four rounds. The correct one is
   298	**hard-invariant validation**: *"Typing and structural validators at the boundary of the system are not
   299	decision points in the sense this principle applies to — they don't evaluate messages, they reject malformed
   300	input. These belong at the API edge and are fine as brittle blockers."* That is exactly what this is — a
   301	structural validator sitting at the API edge, rejecting a payload that carries no content, evaluating
   302	nothing about what the message MEANS. A message of a single full stop passes,
   303	correctly. The lint's authority is a closed-world format invariant at a dev-process chokepoint.
   304	
   305	**Structured decision logging — the requirement this section MISSED until round 11.** `signal-vs-authority.md`
   306	states it plainly: *"Authorities must log their decisions in a structured form: which signals they received,
   307	what the conversation context was, which rule they applied, and what the outcome was."* This guard is
   308	blocking authority and logged nothing. The gap was raised at round 4, not acted on, and raised again at
   309	round 11 — seeing a finding and not acting on it is worse than not seeing it, and it is recorded that way.
   310	Every refusal now emits `{guard, outcome, method, field, rule, valueLength, engine, unicode}` through an
   311	injectable sink, the record is written BEFORE the throw so a caller that swallows the error cannot also
   312	swallow the record, a throwing sink can never convert a refusal into a delivery, and the payload itself is
   313	NEVER logged — length only, because an invisible payload is still user content. The decision also rides on
   314	the error, so a catcher can record it too.
   315	
   316	**On the doc's "conversation context" field, which this record OMITS — reconciled rather than quietly
   317	dropped (round 12).** The requirement exists so over-blocks and under-blocks become detectable. For a gate
   318	that weighs signals against a conversation, context is the evidence you need to judge whether it decided
   319	well. **This predicate is context-FREE by construction:** its verdict depends on the payload and nothing
   320	else, so no amount of conversational context could make an all-invisible payload the right thing to send,
   321	and none could explain a wrong decision. Recording a topic id would make the log look more compliant
   322	without making a single over-block or under-block more detectable. What DOES make them detectable is
   323	already there — the exact method, field, rule and payload length. And the sink is **injectable** precisely
   324	so a host that HAS correlation context can add it at the boundary where that context actually exists,
   325	rather than plumbing a topic id through six senders and a pure predicate to satisfy a field.
   326	
   327	## Decision points touched
   328	
   329	| decision point | classification | justification |
   330	|---|---|---|
   331	| **Refuse an outbound Telegram payload whose reader-visible field has no visible characters** | `invariant` | **Restated after round 1, and REPLACED after round 6.** The predicate is NOT "is this visible to a reader" — that depends on Unicode version, grapheme clustering, emoji modifiers and the rendering client, and is not settled. Nor is it the subtractive question it originally asked, which let eight non-printing categories through. What is closed and decidable is the POSITIVE question the code now asks: *does this string contain at least one letter, number, punctuation mark, symbol, or MARK* (`\p{L}\p{N}\p{P}\p{S}\p{M}`, resolved by the host engine's Unicode tables). **`\p{M}` was added at review passes 30-31**: excluding all marks over-refused real text, and the advance-width rationale for splitting Mn from Mc/Me was measured false on the host and withdrawn — a lone combining mark is content. This paragraph said L/N/P/S/M only until review pass 36 finding 7 caught the spec describing a predicate the code had stopped implementing. Deterministic for a given engine, no competing signal, and closed against categories nobody has thought of yet. There is no competing signal, no context that changes the answer, and no open-domain judgment about meaning — a single full stop passes, correctly. This is the **hard-invariant validation at the API edge** case (see *Signal vs authority* below, where round 12 corrected an earlier mis-citation of the enumerable-inputs exception), not a judgment point wearing an `invariant` label. |
   332	| **Which Telegram methods carry a reader-visible field** | `invariant`, as a **VERSIONED POLICY TABLE** | **Reclassified after round 1.** Calling this "a fact about the API" was wrong twice over: the Bot API can add or change methods, and the `answerCallbackQuery` exclusion is a product-behaviour judgment (an empty toast is *useful*), not a mechanical one. It is an invariant at RUNTIME — a closed code-defined map with no per-call judgment — but it is policy that carries a review trigger, not a timeless truth. **Review trigger:** any Bot API version bump, or any new `apiCall` method reaching a reader. The pinning test is what makes a silent drift impossible. |
   333	| **Whether a source file is a Telegram body-sender (the lint's population)** | `invariant` | Derived mechanically — builds the API host string AND calls `fetch` AND references a body-carrying method. No semantic judgment; a shrink-only ratchet makes a silent population loss loud. |
   334	
   335	> **Contested in both directions, per the standard.** None of the three is a competing-signals point dressed
   336	> as an invariant: none weighs evidence, none has an "it depends" case, and none can be right or wrong about
   337	> intent — only about characters, a code-defined map, and a mechanical file predicate. Conversely, none is
   338	> misfiled as a judgment-candidate: adding an arbiter to "does this string contain content" would be an LLM
   339	> call on a settled question, which *Intelligence Infers* would rightly refuse.
   340	

codex
One additional claim defect is confirmed in the explicit table itself: `divider` is classified and commented as rendering nothing, while Telegram defines it as the `<hr/>` divider block. That does not create the invisible-send bypass—the permit is safe—but it makes the supposedly semantic `NOTHING` state false and is therefore a separate PRECISION finding.
apply patch
patch: completed
/Users/justin/window13-exam/PASS48-VERDICT.md
diff --git a/PASS48-VERDICT.md b/PASS48-VERDICT.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..80aa09189feb2a5f80025d5512b7bc7a19d26e6a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/PASS48-VERDICT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+=== VERDICT ===
+UNSOUND
+
+The pass-47 discriminator table is faithful to `Client.cpp`, and the two priority-union repairs are present. The guarantee still fails in the two other native `InputRichMessage` arms: `markdown` and `html` are reduced with the old scalar regexes rather than Telegram's rich grammar, and a nonempty media declaration is treated as rendered without establishing that the winning source references it. One further table entry makes a false semantic claim about dividers. Thus both the runtime guarantee and the requirement that every supporting claim be true fail.
+
+=== MAGNITUDE ===
+3 NEW load-bearing findings: 2 DESIGN, 1 PRECISION. The 10 previously-stated-open items are tracked separately in REGRESSION-CHECK and are not included in that count.
+
+## FINDINGS
+
+1. **DESIGN — the mathematical-expression repair covers explicit blocks, but not the `markdown` or `html` forms of the same rich-message grammar.**
+
+   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:584-599` turns the entire winning `markdown` or `html` source into a scalar leaf. That leaf goes through `readerVisibleText` at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:352-360`, whose Markdown model removes only ordinary inline links and whose HTML model removes only tags. The result is admitted on the first category-positive source character at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:653-657`. Telegram, however, natively parses inline and block formulas in both rich Markdown and rich HTML, and specifies that formula bodies are raw LaTeX source ([Rich Message Formatting Options](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#rich-message-formatting-options)). The pass-47 reasoning at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:490-493` correctly says source characters are not rendered glyphs, but only `type: "mathematical_expression"` reaches that OPAQUE branch (`src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:416-417,506-512`). The regression at `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:498-518` likewise exercises only the explicit-block arm; `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:324-329` gives the HTML arm only a styled zero-width literal, not rich grammar.
+
+   Concrete failure path: a classified rich-message method receives its JSON object form; the container selects `markdown` or `html`; its only rendered item is a valid layout/spacing-only formula. The guard sees the LaTeX source's letters or punctuation as visible, returns at line 657, and `telegramFetch` reaches `fetch` at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:420,435`. Telegram parses those source characters as formula instructions and the reader receives no glyph. This is not a recount of the already-declared general CMT-1260 scalar-formatting limitation: it is the specifically claimed pass-47 formula repair missing two of the three native `InputRichMessage` representations.
+
+2. **DESIGN — a media *definition* vouches for the message even when the winning source does not use it.**
+
+   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:589` defines `mediaPresent` as any nonempty array; `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:591-599` copies that boolean to `undecidable`; and `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:655` unconditionally allows it before examining the leaves. The comment at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:581-582` says presence renders. The API defines the array instead as media **specified in** the `markdown` or `html` source using an id-bearing `tg://...` link ([InputRichMessage](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#inputrichmessage)). The Bot API parser likewise passes source and media as separate inputs (`Client.cpp:11633-11640` in the [official server](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/master/telegram-bot-api/Client.cpp)); TDLib retains the source and media table separately (`RichMessage.cpp:98-117`) and derives received rich-message media from the parsed blocks (`RichMessage.cpp:75-79`) in the [official implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tdlib/td/master/td/telegram/RichMessage.cpp). Presence is a declaration, not proof of a rendered reference.
+
+   Concrete failure path: the winning `markdown` or `html` source contains only mechanically invisible rendered text, while `media` contains a syntactically valid entry whose id is never referenced by that source. The guard returns solely because the array is nonempty. The server parses the winning source without a media block, so the unused definition contributes nothing to the reader; the remaining text also renders as nothing. This recreates pass 47's discarded-member-vouching defect one layer above the discriminator table.
+
+3. **PRECISION — the table says a divider renders nothing, but a divider is itself a rendered horizontal rule.**
+
+   Evidence: `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:453-454` places both `divider` and `anchor` in `BLOCK_RENDERS_NOTHING` and comments that both render nothing. Telegram defines `InputRichBlockDivider` as a divider corresponding to `<hr/>` ([Bot API, InputRichBlockDivider](https://core.telegram.org/bots/api#inputrichblockdivider)); only the anchor is a non-rendering jump target. `blockScan` therefore returns the `NOTHING` state at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:535` for a block that visibly renders.
+
+   Concrete failure path: an explicit-block rich message containing a divider enters `blockScan`, is classified as proved-empty, then is allowed by the no-leaf return at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:656`; the reader sees a rule. The runtime decision happens to be safe for the one-way guarantee, but the table comment and the meaning assigned to `NOTHING` are false. This is load-bearing precision: closing the stated-open no-leaf allowance by trusting this state would refuse real rendered content, and the current table cannot support the claimed `NOTHING`/OPAQUE distinction.
+
+## REGRESSION-CHECK
+
+- **Redirect crossing — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:36-45` states the judgment; method/host classification uses only the initial `href` at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:313,328-358`, and the final native fetch at line 435 still follows redirects.
+- **Body encoding inferred from the wrapper — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** `collectParams` branches on JavaScript body type/content at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:215-275`; it never consults `Content-Type`.
+- **Any duplicated top-level JSON key makes the whole body unreadable — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** Duplicate detection and refusal occur at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:217-230`, before field relevance is selected at lines 383-420.
+- **`answerCallbackQuery` and `editForumTopic` — CONFIRMED AS PRODUCT JUDGMENTS, not new.** Their opposite classifications remain at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:121-123,159-160,186-208`; neither is derived mechanically.
+- **Local Bot API Server — CONFIRMED OUTSIDE BOTH RECOGNISERS, not new.** Runtime hard-codes `api.telegram.org` at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:60,92-93`; lint hard-codes the same literal at `scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:51-55,143-147`.
+- **Multipart and single-read prose claims — CONFIRMED FALSE, not new.** `FormData` is replaced with a URL-encoded string and non-string parts are dropped at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:319-326`, so the sent body is not multipart. `{ ...init }` at line 433 re-reads a `body` getter before line 434 overwrites it, contradicting the no-second-read claim at lines 428-432 even though the captured body is ultimately the one sent.
+- **Structured content in query/form/multipart — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** Those encodings remain scalar strings at `src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:207-269`; only object-valued fields enter `structuredFieldScan` at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:653-654`. The query regression at `tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:409-415` even calls a serialized rich structure “checked” although it remains a string.
+- **Lint host prefilter uses literal source text — CONFIRMED OPEN, not new.** Files without the literal substring are skipped at `scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:143-147`; the categorical success output remains at lines 253-255, subject to the limitations admitted at lines 23-27.
+- **No-leaf structures are allowed — CONFIRMED OPEN AND NOT CARRIED FAR ENOUGH, not new.** `NOTHING` and `OPAQUE` are distinct scan values at `src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:388-400`, but enforcement permits both at lines 655-656. A proved-non-rendering anchor is therefore still allowed, and opaque content is also an unconditional waiver. Finding 3 separately records the new fact that `divider` was put in the wrong side of that distinction.
+- **Deliberately red family audit — CONFIRMED, not new.** `tests/unit/standards-coverage-ratchet.test.ts:906-925` deliberately expects all audits current while Building and The Substrate remain stale; `docs/specs/reports/window12-closeout.md:98-109` names the required real multi-reviewer convergence and rejects editing the expectation.

